        "root_concepts": "[carbonates, phosphates]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Carbonates and Phosphates",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nDOLOMITE: CaMg(CO₃)₂ — ordered alternation of Ca and Mg layers\n  Rhombohedral; very similar to calcite but Mg-rich\n  Reacts with HCl only when powdered or in hot acid (not cold → test!)\n  \"The dolomite problem\": how does dolomite form?\n    Primary dolomite precipitation is kinetically inhibited at low T\n    Most ancient dolomite = secondary (dolomitization of calcite)\n    by Mg²⁺-rich brine replacing Ca²⁺: CaCO₃ + Mg²⁺ → CaMg(CO₃)₂ + Ca²⁺\n  Major reservoir rock for oil and gas (dolomite porosity)\n  Named for Dolomieu (French geologist); the Dolomites (mountains) named\n    for the mineral, which is unusually prominent there"
    },
